  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 219 PM MDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nNorthwesterly flow is favored overhead at 500-mb Wednesday morning \naccording to GEFS and EC ensembles. A shortwave trough embedded in \nthe northwesterly flow is forecast to move through the area \nthroughout the day. GEFS and EC members are in support of a surface \nlow at the leading edge of this shortwave already past the forecast \narea by morning, which would favor a northerly component to the\nsurface winds. High temperatures are favored to be cooler than \nTuesday, in the low to mid 80s. In addition, thunderstorms are \npossible Wednesday ahead of a secondary cold front. NBM 24 hr \nprecipitation guidance shows the best chances for greater than \n0.1 inches of rain across Eastern Colorado and Southwestern \nNebraska, with some areas at a 50-60% chance. These \nthunderstorms would be supported by a modest 40-50 kt jet streak\nat 500-mb, particularly across northern portions of the \nforecast area. LREF guidance is in support of this solution, \nshowing a two in three chance that sfc-500mb wind shear will \nexceed 30 kts for areas along and north of I-70, and as high as \nan 80-90% chance across Northeast Colorado and Southwest \nNebraska. As such, severe thunderstorms, particularly across \nthis zone where wind shear would be highest, are possible. The \nfavored mode for these storms are currently clusters and/or line\nsegments, as supported by vertical wind shear parallel to the \nincoming cold front alongside recent RRFS data showing a cluster\nand/or line segment coming through the area during the \nevening/overnight hours. The primary hazard with these storms \nwould be strong winds, with a secondary hazard of large hail. \nConfidence in severe weather occurring is highest north of I-70,\naround 10%. \n\nNorthwesterly flow is forecast to persist into Thursday, as ridging \ncontinues to build off to the west. While surface winds may still be \nout of the northeast during the day, they are forecast to mainly \nstay below 20 mph. This would allow sufficient warming to take place \nThursday afternoon as long as clouds clear out. Otherwise, \ntemperatures would be able to remain cooler Thursday. Forecast highs \nare in the mid to upper 80s, though could reach into the 90s if \nsufficient heating can take place. Showers may develop during the \nevening hours, though NBM 24 hr precipitation guidance suggests less \nthan a 20% chance for greater than 0.1 inches of rain across nearly \nthe entire forecast area. LREF guidance continues to show a two in \nthree chance that vertical wind shear will be above 30 kts along and \nnorth of I-70 Thursday, which may allow some storms to become\nsevere. However, given the anticipated low coverage of storms\nThursday, confidence in severe weather occurring is 5% or less.\n\nThe remainder of the forecast period looks to be dominated by warmer \ntemperatures as ridging from the west moves in overhead. Highs \nFriday through Sunday are forecast in the 90s. Conditions are also \nforecast to be drier, as NBM 24 hr precipitation guidance continues \nto show a 20% chance or less for greater than 0.1 inches of rain \nacross the forecast area. Additionally, relative humidities (RH) may \ndrop a little bit with the increase in temperatures, but are not \nforecast to drop below 20%. We may see RH values drop further into \nthe teens if southwesterly surface flow can develop across the area. \nThis would be possible given the persisting northwesterly upper-\nlevel flow to the north and east of the forecast region, which would \nallow surface lows to track across this area. Even so, fire weather \nconditions are not expected, as wind gusts are forecast to remain \nbelow 25 mph all three days. However, if RH values can drop into the \nteens and a few thundershowers could develop, there would be a risk \nfor dry lightning to start fires.",
